Document Hierarchy & Single Source of Truth (Mandatory)
Apply on every decision and every edit.

The authoritative statement of these rules is docs/SPEC.md §0; this is a working copy for tooling.

Hierarchy (highest → lowest)
1. docs/SPEC.md — product rules, domain logic, API contracts, acceptance criteria, scope
2. docs/ARCHITECTURE.md — system structure, modules, messaging, persistence, ports, technical boundaries
3. docs/GLOSSARY.md — all naming (types, fields, flags, statuses, endpoints, components)

Conflict Resolution
- Higher document always prevails. Align or ignore lower documents.
- Requests that conflict with SPEC.md (out-of-scope or constraints) → do not implement. Record under “Future work”. Stay strictly in scope.
- Never invent rules, names, modules, or steps that contradict any document.
- When uncertain: re-read the highest relevant document and quote the exact rule before acting.

Anti-Duplication
- Product / domain / API / acceptance / scope → ONLY SPEC.md
- Structure / modules / messaging / persistence / ports → ONLY ARCHITECTURE.md
- Naming → ONLY GLOSSARY.md

Forbidden
- Copy any rule, threshold, flag, status logic, naming, or architecture decision into another file.
- Lower documents may only reference higher documents (e.g. “see SPEC.md §4”). Never restate content.

Document Change Protocol
1. Edit only the authoritative file.
2. Immediately scan the other two files for stale or duplicated content.
3. Remove or update so the three files remain non-overlapping and consistent.
4. A change to a higher file that invalidates a lower file must be fixed in the same response.

Pre-Completion Check (docs tasks)
- No rule or name is defined in more than one file.
- All three files are mutually consistent.

Project Context
Informatics student portfolio project for agriculture-tech job applications.
Student must explain every part of the codebase in interviews.
Optimize for clarity and teachability, not cleverness.

Interview-First Coding
- Prefer simple, readable code the student can walk through on a whiteboard.
- Prefer clear names over short names.
- Prefer straightforward control flow over clever abstractions.
- Do not use advanced patterns unless they already exist in the repo and are necessary.
- Do not add layers for “enterprise best practice” (extra interfaces, factories, event buses, DI frameworks beyond Spring’s defaults).
- Keep methods short and purpose-obvious.
- Do not add new languages, frameworks, brokers, or databases beyond modules listed in ARCHITECTURE.md.

Working Rules
- Make the smallest change that completes the requested task.
- Match existing package style (com.mariolagger.*), DTOs, and constructor injection.
- Do not implement Icebox / out-of-scope items from SPEC.
- If unsure, choose the option easier to explain in an interview.
- If any requirement, detail, decision, edge case, or implementation choice is not fully understood, ask multiple-choice questions covering every uncertainty before making any changes. Prefer the maximum number of questions needed; more questions are better than assumptions. Do not implement until all answers are received and understanding is complete.

Response Style
Prefer brevity. Avoid unnecessary explanations, restatements, or filler unless the user explicitly asks for more detail.